WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.716
Logs help administrators and analysts understand what happened on a Linux system.

2
00:00:05.716 --> 00:00:17.147
They may record authentication attempts, sudo use, service starts and stops, kernel messages, package changes, scheduled tasks, application errors, firewall decisions, and security alerts.

3
00:00:17.147 --> 00:00:20.481
Without logs, teams are often left guessing.

4
00:00:20.481 --> 00:00:27.625
With useful logs, they can build a timeline, troubleshoot failures, confirm changes, and support investigation.

5
00:00:27.625 --> 00:00:30.959
Authentication and sudo events are especially valuable.

6
00:00:30.959 --> 00:00:41.914
Failed logins, unexpected successful logins, new privileged access, repeated sudo failures, or administrative activity outside a normal change window can all deserve review.

7
00:00:41.914 --> 00:00:45.248
These signals do not automatically prove misuse.

8
00:00:45.248 --> 00:00:54.297
They help analysts ask better questions, compare activity with approved changes, and identify when a system needs closer attention.

9
00:00:54.297 --> 00:00:58.584
Monitoring turns logs and system signals into operational awareness.

10
00:00:58.584 --> 00:01:09.539
It can help detect disk pressure, service failures, unexpected process behavior, configuration drift, package changes, time synchronization problems, backup failures, and suspicious activity.

11
00:01:09.539 --> 00:01:13.349
Monitoring should be tuned to the system's purpose.

12
00:01:13.349 --> 00:01:22.399
A noisy alert that nobody owns is less useful than a small set of signals with clear response expectations.

13
00:01:22.399 --> 00:01:24.304
Logs are also sensitive.

14
00:01:24.304 --> 00:01:32.877
They may contain usernames, IP addresses, application data, paths, errors, and details that reveal how a system works.

15
00:01:32.877 --> 00:01:35.735
Logs need retention, protection, and review.

16
00:01:35.735 --> 00:01:42.403
They should not be writable by unnecessary users, deleted too quickly, or exposed broadly.

17
00:01:42.403 --> 00:01:53.834
A good audit trail supports troubleshooting, accountability, and recovery because it records what changed, when it changed, and who or what performed the action.
